[{:seon.task/name "a-branch-head-is-a-store-a-render-derefs"
  :seon.task/plan "Web's (agreed with context-4, 2026-11-14), after the reaction core lands.\n\nShape: a branch's head is (seon.ratom/store db) per branch, kept by the render proc, which owns its reads. Its commit! is fed by that branch's commit notices (seon.writer/a-branch-commit-sends-its-datoms, runtime's). It is the same Store and the same key-set match as main's, with no second mechanism.\n\nA component that derefs a branch's store records that branch's datom keys and re-runs only on that branch's commits that touch them.\n\nDeletes: the render proc's ask-turns, turns! and branch-turn, and the {:op :branch-db} asks for turns. seon.render.ui/turns-tile draws my.root/turns-of's rows by deref, so the turns ledger is one implementation in two views.\n\nUsers: the turns tile, and seon.render.ui/roots-page-shows-each-branchs-drift-from-main.\n\nEstimate: a branch commit costs its datoms matched against the readers' key sets (O(datoms moved)). No branch head is read whole, and nothing is asked.\n\nProof: seon.render-test/a-component-reading-a-branch-reruns-on-its-commit. It reruns on a commit of a datom it read on branch b, not on one of branch c, and not on b's commit of a datom it didn't read.\n\nCatch-up [M]: the branch Store reuses advance-main's rule as is (render/proc.clj:852-865). On each notice it reads the head at :commit, diffs from the head it holds (ctx/moved, O(changed)), and calls commit! with those datoms. A db no newer changes nothing. A dropped notice is covered by the next one's diff, so the Store never reads :ops.\n\nChannels, checked 17:15 [M, system.edn:50-56 and 95-99]: the notice comes on the writer's :branches out port into render's :branches in port, as a sliding buffer of one (:bind/render-branches-buf, next to render-main-buf). One port carries many branches, so a notice can evict another branch's. The reader therefore treats any notice as \"some branch moved\": it reads the head commit id of each branch it holds (O(B) pointer reads), and diffs and commit!s only the Stores whose head moved. The newest notice always arrives, so no branch's move is lost. A reader needing every commit takes a bounded lossless buffer, as the reconciler does on :main.\n\nReading heads, checked 17:30 [M, writer.clj:353-361, render/proc.clj:1107] against runtime's Flow rules (side-session-notes runtime-flow-survey-2026-11-14.md):\n- The render proc never reads the store itself. On a :branches-moved notice it asks the writer {:op :branch-db :branch b} for each branch it holds, through the existing ask with its :asking guard (proc.clj:1107: one ask in flight per branch, never a second).\n- The writer owns the conn and answers {:db head :base :base-t}. The answer commit!s that branch's Store with the datoms-diff from the head it held.\n- Nothing in a step parks on another proc, and no reaction blocks. The Stores live in the render proc's own state, not one port per branch, since ports are fixed at init.\n\nRevised 21:20 with runtime-2 [M, main's proc.clj:1120 and 1148]: an agent's :commit event already records [:commits agent], and ask-branches includes it in its at, so the next pass re-asks {:op :branch-db} for that branch. The writer notice would be a second way, so seon.writer/a-branch-commit-sends-its-datoms is cancelled. A branch Store here is fed by that answer: advance! from the head it held, as the core's seon.ratom/advance! does for main (context-4's name). This task needs nothing new from runtime. It does need context-4's advance! and its key index, which come after Stage 0."
  :seon.task/kind :whole
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-component-reading-a-branch-reruns-on-its-commit}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-whole-health-read-never-holds-the-render-proc"
  :seon.task/plan "Web's. Found 2026-11-14 21:00-21:20 [M].\n- The standing root (pid 77011) ran at 99% CPU for 54+ min, and :render answered no Flow ping (flow), so it was inside one step.\n- seon.problems/health whole on live main takes 80.4 s, 77.7 s of it seon.problems/hand-written-lists (context's cost, filed with context).\n- seon.render.proc/health! reads health whole inside the render proc's step whenever it holds no report or its held point doesn't match a commit's t (proc.clj:781-784), and so does the :health handler while no root walk exists (proc.clj:1297-1301).\n- The render proc is :mixed, one OS thread, so every page and every :health ask waits behind that step: hazard 1 of docs/flow.md, a long step wedging a proc.\n\nThe class: a derived read of unbounded cost inside a proc's step. Change:\n- The whole read becomes an effect the render proc asks, answered on its own in-port as the writer's :branch-db is. It is asked once when no report is held, and never a second while one is in flight (the :asking guard).\n- Meanwhile the proc keeps answering pages and :health from the report it holds, marked with the point it was read at.\n- health-moved stays in the step: it costs what moved (8.2 M ticks for a one-run change, measured in its docstring).\n- The :health handler answers the held report at its point, and the api's caller reads :at to see how fresh it is. It never waits on a whole read inside the step.\n\nEstimate: a render step's health work is O(moved) via health-moved; the whole read runs at most once per boot, or per held-point loss, off the step.\n\nProofs:\n- seon.render-test/a-whole-health-read-runs-off-the-render-step: while a whole read is in flight, a :page request is answered from the held state, without waiting for it;\n- seon.agent-test/root-reads-the-health-the-render-proc-keeps within its 3 M tick estimate again, once context's class is at its bound.\n\nNeeds: nothing to start; context's cost fix makes the whole read itself cheap.\n\nRuled by root, 20:47: the whole read moves off the step as a reaction over the store (the core, 29e96b1f60), not a new effect kind. Main's health is a reaction whose reads are recorded. The render proc derefs its held value, so a class that runs long delays only its own value, and a move of main re-runs only what it read (health-moved's job, done by the core's key-set match). This supersedes the effect design above.\nMeasured 20:58 [M]: :render answered neither of two Flow pings 2 s apart, still mid-step; its state is only readable after the step ends. The cost itself was scan-form's :: lookbehind regex (O(n²)), now seon.problems/unaliased in one pass (context's Land A, 4c386d46d3): hand-written-lists over every var on live main went from 77.7 s to 1,368 ms and 929 MB, 544 rows [M, 21:45].\n\nSeam agreed with context-4, 21:10:\n- Context-4's classes stay plain fns of a db and its answers; seon.problems/health composes them.\n- The render proc holds main as an rv/store. advance-main feeds commit! with the datoms-diff it already computes from the main it holds, never a notice's ops, so render's :main stays a sliding buffer of one and is never a slow tap on the writer's mult.\n- Health, and each class inside it, is a track over that store. rv/*tracks* is bound to the cache the render proc's state holds, around each step that derefs.\n- Anything a class reads outside the store (the api's fold, config, files) is a track argument.\n- health-moved and health!'s point and fold bookkeeping go only with a proof that the tracks answer what a whole read answers after a commit; Deletes: names them.\n- Built after context-4's :form/lists lands.\nProof added: seon.render-test/the-health-tracks-answer-what-a-whole-read-does.\n\nAnalysis before code (owner via root, 21:46), from measurements on live main 2026-11-14 [M] unless marked:\n- n: ~17 health classes (runtime's map), over ~6,000 forms, ~700 tasks and 2,312 error entities.\n- Per class whole (ms, at root's REPL): answers 4, report 420, other-derived 1,734 (several classes together), unnamed-sites 288, stale-sections 204 (247 recorded, +20%), hand-written-lists ~1,000 [H, after Land A and deps' regex fix].\n- First run: the sum, ~3.7 s ≈ 1.6e9 ticks at ~4.2e8 ticks/s, once per boot.\n- Live memory: ~135 B a key (runtime T9); stale-sections holds 1,522 keys, ~205 KB; ~17 classes ≈ 3.5 MB [H: other classes' key sets unmeasured].\n- Per commit: O(classes whose keys it touches × that class's cost). A task or agent fact touches report and answers (~0.45 s); a form commit touches hand-written-lists, unnamed-sites and stale-sections (~1.5 s); an unrelated commit reruns none (0 ms, T2).\n- Budgets in the proofs: ticks per commit = the touched classes' sum (≤ 2x); bytes = the key sets (≤ 4 MB); seconds = ticks / 4.2e8, never a wall-clock assertion.\n- Gain over today: a whole read on every health! that can't move its held report (80 s, ~1.5 s after the fixes) becomes zero for untouched classes.\n\nAdoption of the owner step (runtime-2, 21:58; plan in notes runtime-core-map/plan-advance-heights-owner-step.md): the render proc binds seon.ratom/*owner* around its step. The core then holds auto-run reactions to the owner's end-of-step flush, in height order, which fixes the diamond glitch [M, runtime A1: top 3 → 1]. So components re-render once per step, at its flush, never inside each commit. Proof: a commit whose datoms reach one component by two paths (a health class and the banner deriving from it) reruns it once in the step's :ran."
  :seon.task/kind :whole
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-whole-health-read-runs-off-the-render-step seon.render-test/the-health-tracks-answer-what-a-whole-read-does}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-page-keeps-its-renders-css-on-every-pass"
  :seon.task/plan "No render's own CSS (:seon.render/style, drawn by seon.render.ui/page-rules into #p-rules) reaches the live page: 0 <style> on /agent/root and every agent page [M browser and curl, 2026-11-15]. Narrowed [M]: a cold walk of root's page draws the page-rules unit (6,582 B) and the stage unit holds its <!--c:p-rules--> marker (results c81e47cc, d0fdcbcf); agent-view's units hold it; a FIRST pass-view of [:chat :root] on a fresh state composes 21 <style>, with and without root's system walk (results c55bee58, b79d9fdd). So it is lost on the live render proc's later passes (an advanced kept walk or a view held from before), never on a first draw. Next: reproduce two passes with a move between (cheap now the chat walk draws no model request), find the pass that drops the unit, fix it at its cause, red first. Cause unread at both ends until then.\n\nUpdate [M browser 14:35]: since 470ebf9c5d (a chat's walk draws no model request) the live /agent/root carries all 21 <style> and the page-rules unit, after a reply's update pass. So the later passes lost it while the walk drew the model output [H: the two outputs' placement of page-rules, render!'s :for-only]. The proof below pins it across a move; if it holds green on main, this task closes with the proof as its guard. Cites: `seon.render.ui/page-rules`, `seon.render.proc/branch-walk`, `docs/render.md#style`."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.render.proc{pass-view {:n 100, :order :linear, :ticks 2000000, :bytes 4000000}}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-page-keeps-its-rules-across-a-move}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-health-asks-wait-is-driven-and-counted"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime; reviewer flow (approved 2026-11-16, on seon.render.proc/a-health-ask-waits-for-settling-never-loops-in-the-step, jolt/runtime 8a4d2f72ca). Conditions for that build: (1) waiting :health asks are residue: :seon.flow/held names them as their own key beside the queue size ({:queue n :waiting m}), so a drain never sees 0 with asks unanswered; (2) something is always in flight while an ask waits: an ask that arrives unsettled while health-more has nothing queued makes the :health step emit the next bounded health-more step itself; (3) an ask is answered by the first settle at or after the point it advanced main to, never an earlier one, through the step's outputs, never a blocking put; (4) a stop answers waiting asks typed, named for the proc (:seon.render.proc/stopped). PROOF, red first: an ask on an idle, unsettled report is answered."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render.proc-test/a-health-ask-on-an-idle-unsettled-report-is-answered}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.render.proc{health-step {:n 1, :order :constant, :ticks 100000, :bytes 50000}}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}]